<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achaeans (Homer)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Achaeans are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>; it is the poet's most common collective name, alongside Danaans and Argives, for the army that besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>. Many scholars connect the name with Ahhiyawa, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> kingdom mentioned in Hittite texts and generally placed in Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In later times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> was the name of a region of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achaeans-homer.jpg" alt="Achaeans (Homer)" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sosias (potter, signed). Painting attributed to the Sosias Painter (name piece for Beazley, overriding attribution) or the … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akhilleus_Patroklos_Antikensammlung_Berlin_F2278.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
